  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAs continues to be highlighted, we are anticipating an active \npattern through midweek with passing disturbances in the broad \nupper trough lending to lift in the vicinity of a nearly \nstationary boundary that will set up in the upper Ohio Valley. \nSeveral rounds of showers and storms are anticipated in the W-NW\nflow, with the first potentially arriving overnight Sunday night\ninto Monday morning.\n\nAn ongoing MCS in western Ohio Sunday evening is continuing to\nproduce measured severe wind gusts as well as severe hail. Model\nguidance has continued to suggest that this complex weakens \nand brings little to no impact locally, but it has prevailed \nand mesoanalysis suggests that it may not spare our area\nentirely. A tight NW to SE oriented theta-E gradient has been \nhovering west of our CWA for most of the day, and model \nexpectation was that the MCS would dive southeast along that\nfavorable gradient while the northeastward extent fizzles out. \nHowever, stronger southwesterly flow has strengthened theta-E \nadvection and pushed that gradient farther northeast. Because \nof that, the MCS has prevailed in a more easterly direction and \nis beginning to raise some concern about its maintenance post- \nsunset and impacts locally. Highest probability for impacts in \nour area should be in eastern OH and will be in the form of \ndamaging wind and marginally severe hail, though the wind should\nbe predominant as the cluster congeals with cold pools. Lesser\nconfidence lies farther to the east into western PA and whether\nor not it can survive that far and that long after sunset. With\nthat said, the nocturnal stable layer won't be notably strong\ntonight, so even with some MLCIN settling in, that may not be\nenough to kill it entirely. Bottom line is we're operating on\na mesoscale-type timeframe with overall low confidence in\nevolution.\n\nIf more widespread coverage of storms prevails Sunday night into\nthe pre-dawn hours Monday morning, regeneration of additional \nstorms likely is delayed much later in the day on Monday. \nHowever, the alternative scenario is that this convective \ncomplex dies off/follows a southward track away from our area \nand leaves an outflow for convective initiation in the late \nmorning which some other model guidance suggests. Regardless, \nthere appears to be a period of time Monday where there is \nsufficient shear and instability for strong/severe storms. \nDamaging wind gusts remain the primary threat, but hail and \ntornado risks also are present. In addition, given the continued\npresence of above- normal precipitable water values (90% of \nclimo), as well as the potential for some training storms along \nthe boundary, WPC continues to highlight our region in a Slight \n(level 2 of 4) Risk for excessive rain for Monday into Monday \nnight. \n\nSevere/heavy rain risks continue for Tuesday and Wednesday with \nfocus along the aforementioned wavering boundary. Machine-learning \nguidance is in support of this. There is some suggestion in a slight \nsouthwestward shift in the heaviest precipitation from the runs \nyesterday, which could spare some of the region from the axis of \nheaviest rainfall but this will need to be monitored. The stronger \nGreat Lakes shortwave may finally push the boundary definitively \nsouth of the region by Thursday/Friday, leading to less threatening \nweather.",
